<p>ElevationLab has withdrawn its AirTag Silencer from sale. The device precisely destroyed the speaker in an AirTag while leaving tracking functions intact. Two weeks ago, <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/10/silencing-airtags-enables-stalkers-and-domestic-violence/">I strongly suggested it stop selling it</a> because their precision product offered abusers and stalkers a useful tool—true, available from other sources, but none with the high production quality or the stellar reputation among Apple users that ElevationLab has.</p>
<p>The company sent out a brief email to customers today that contained the same text as <a href="https://www.elevationlab.com/blogs/news/an-apology">a blog entry</a> titled, &#8220;An apology&#8221;:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  We launched an AirTag silencing tool recently. We’ve always made products we want for ourselves, and this was no different &#8211; we wanted it for AirTags on our bikes. (Some of us have bikes more valuable than our cars.)</p>
<p>  In retrospect though, this also has the side effect of aiding someone with bad intentions to track someone that wouldn’t get phone notifications.</p>
<p>  We have removed the product and will use our patent on it to prevent anyone else from making one in the future.
</p></blockquote>
<p>At the time, the company defended the product. I don&#8217;t know if my words had any effect.</p>
